    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's take a look at Life Expectancy in different countries, and
                how factors contribute to Life Expectancy.
                Download the "Life Expectancy Data.csv" file on your computer,
                and upload the data file into Colab as a dataframe, following
                the procedure we used for the Penguins Data.
                Then complete the following steps.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If you notice, the data has multiple years for most
                    countries.
                    We'll want to just focus on the latest year, 2015.
                    Create a new dataframe where the <c>Year</c> is only the
                    year 2015.
                    Show the new dataframe below - make sure you give it a new
                    name.
                    (It will not show all the rows as there are too many, that's
                    fine.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Notice the data has a lot of NaN values, meaning we're
                    missing data.
                    But if we drop all the NaN's now, we might lose more data
                    than we want.
                    Let's only keep a few of the columns that we'll want to
                    look at before we worry about that.
                    Make a new dataframe from the dataframe with the only year
                    2015, and only keep the columns <c>Country</c>,
                    <c>LifeExpectancy</c>, <c>BMI</c>, <c>GDP</c>, and
                    <c>Schooling</c>.
                    Show the new dataframe below.
                    (Make sure you give it a new name.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Drop all the NaN values.
                    How many data points do you have in your new dataset?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The following code is a really cool interactive graph that
                    helps us click on data points and read off the information
                    for that data point.
                    Since we have so many countries, it will help us isolate a
                    country and its data.
                    You can hover over a data point to see the information, or
                    you can double click on the country in the legend to isolate
                    that country first, then hover over the data point to see
                    the data values.
                    (Double clicking on the legend again gets back to the
                    original graph.)
                </p>

                <p>
                    Adapt the code below to use your new dataframe (below it's
                    called df), to plot BMI vs Life Expectancy.
                    Create two additional plots - one to plot GDP vs Life
                    Expectancy, and one to plot Schooling vs Life Expectancy.
                    Which looks like it will be the best predictor of Life
                    Expectancy?
                    Explain your reasoning.
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <code>
import math
